// A linked tree's sort and its orders go over the keys in use, not over
// every key the world could hold (lib/Ent/Transforms/LowerToLoops.cpp,
// treeGoesOverKeysInUse, forTreeKeys): the slots handed out so far, or,
// where no entity is ever destroyed, each archetype's rows so far. The
// trees of five other tests, each in a world made for 16 million entities
// of which it has a dozen or a few hundred: what they print is what they
// print in a world of their own size, on one core and on all, also where
// entities are spawned and connected after an order was made (a key
// handed out since reads no place in it: a place is kept as one more than
// it is). And each holds little memory: every key gone over, the least of
// them held 260 MB and the most 650 MB.

// RUN: sed -E 's/^default_capacity [0-9]+/default_capacity 16777216/' \
// RUN:     %S/Inputs/tree_follow.ent > %t.tree_follow.ent
// RUN: grep -q "^default_capacity 16777216" %t.tree_follow.ent
// RUN: %ent run %S/Inputs/tree_follow.ent 2>/dev/null > %t.tree_follow.own
// RUN: %ent build %t.tree_follow.ent -o %t.tree_follow.bin 2>/dev/null
// RUN: %t.tree_follow.bin 2>/dev/null | cmp - %t.tree_follow.own
// RUN: %python %S/Inputs/peak_mb.py 100 %t.tree_follow.bin
// RUN: %ent run --parallel %S/Inputs/tree_follow.ent 2>/dev/null > %t.tree_follow.all
// RUN: %ent run --parallel %t.tree_follow.ent 2>/dev/null | cmp - %t.tree_follow.all

